// @reject: imports precede every statement in an executable file. The rule is
// @reject: what both Rust parsers always enforced — the loop that reads the
// @reject: leading run of `<#` stops at the first statement — but it was written
// @reject: in no document, and the browser engine accepted an import anywhere
// @reject: because it parsed `Import` as an ordinary statement. So a program
// @reject: that broke the rule ran in exactly one engine of three, and nothing
// @reject: said which was right (DM-12).
// @reject: The rule now has a message that names it instead of naming the token
// @reject: (it used to be `unexpected token: ModuleImport`), and it is written in
// @reject: REFERENCE.md L40 and GUIDE.md.
